nd a union bound over initial colorings shows that the probability majority dynamics from a random start lands on any *specific* matching-type cut is exponentially small per configuration. Even exp(Θ(n)) such configurations can be collectively unreachable if each has an exponentially small basin, and the entropy of the configuration count (Θ(n)) is plausibly outweighed by the basin penalty. Similarly, the claim that the final cut size is Θ(n) with high probability is not established by the critic — the existence of many stable cuts does not show the dynamics typically settles on one. In sum: the critic has correctly falsified the exp(-c n) rarity clause, which I concede as stated; but the consensus conclusion (probability tending to 1) remains untouched, because it depends on basin properties the critic has not addressed, and no invented fact about basins should be asserted on either side without evidence."
